# mem_accum_block.tkr — the C-route regressor of an if-arm block-local SLICE accumulator (0.3.1
# transicao-move-on-return-e-seletor-n-niveis §1/§2, crumbs A2 + A3).
#
# WHAT IT PINS. A `mut xs = list::empty()` grown by top-of-block self-appends inside an `if` arm is
# EXCLUDED from block-local today (the C3 finding: the total read count sees the self-append base but
# the inside count skips the top-of-block assign). Crumb A2 makes `count_block_local_reads` count a
# self-append of the SAME name, so the accumulator is proven block-local; crumb A3's N-level selector
# then routes each `list::push` grow to the arm's OWN region via `tk_slice_push_r(<arm region>, …)`,
# and the region dies at the arm's exit edge.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES. `accumulate` fingerprints its arm-scoped accumulator to 7 (elements 10/20/30 read
# back via non-escaping index comparisons), then `churn` opens and closes four further if-arm-scoped
# accumulators that recycle the freed region memory (8 hits), then an ESCAPING closure built by
# `make_counter` is invoked AFTER the churn (M2c: `add(100)` = 100). The fingerprint `115` (7 + 8 +
# 100) witnesses that the arm buffer read back exactly, the churn recycled cleanly, and the escaping
# closure's captured env survived to the post-return call — a use-after-drop drops the weight.
#
# C ROUTE by construction: `Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]` pins the shared build to the C backend,
# the route crumbs A2/A3 activate the scope routing on.

Feature: mem_accum_block — an if-arm block-local accumulator and an escaping closure both read back exactly

  Scenario: the arm accumulator + churn + escaping closure fingerprint to 115
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "115"
